The code statements that lead to the bug are:
queue->tx_head = 0;
queue->tx_tail = 0;
Reason:
After a link down/up event (e.g., suspend), the hardware resets its TX buffer queue pointer to the base address (index 0). The driver then unconditionally resets `tx_head` and `tx_tail` to 0, discarding any packets still in the TX ring. This causes three problems: (1) pending skbs are lost (memory leak); (2) concurrent updates from `macb_tx_poll()` or `macb_start_xmit()` race with the reset; (3) if a descriptor with `TX_USED` set (already transmitted but not yet processed) lies at the old tail, after reset `tx_head == tx_tail` makes the poll routine think there is no work, blocking transmission until a new packet overwrites that descriptor. The reset occurs without any protection against concurrent access or any reordering of the ring to match the hardware pointer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
// In macb_mac_link_up: remove the two reset lines and instead call:
gem_shuffle_tx_rings(bp);
// Inside gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring (core logic):
spin_lock_irqsave(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
...
shift = tail % ring_size;
...
// rotate descriptors and skbs so tail becomes index 0
...
queue->tx_head = count;
queue->tx_tail = 0;
...
spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
Reason:
Instead of blindly resetting the pointers, the fix shuffles the TX ring (descriptors and their associated skbs) so that the first unsent packet (old `tail`) becomes the first entry of the ring (index 0). This matches the hardware’s reset behavior (pointer goes to base address). The shuffle is performed under the `tx_ptr_lock` spinlock to prevent races with `macb_tx_poll()` and `macb_start_xmit()`. After the rotation, `tx_head` is set to the number of pending packets (`count`) and `tx_tail` to 0, so the software state correctly reflects the hardware pointer. A write memory barrier (`wmb()`) ensures descriptor updates are visible to the hardware before transmission is re‑enabled.

